  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ is increasing for $x\\,\\in \\,($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,,\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,)$",
  "worked_solution": "The function is increasing where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)>0.$ \n\nPreviously, we found that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x)=-3{x}^{2}-6x+24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n-3{x}^{2}-6x+24>0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n-3(x+4)(x-2)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=-3(x+4)(x-2)$ has roots at $x=-4$ and $x=2,$ as shown below:\n\n![](@asset-0@)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is increasing for $x\\in (-4,2).$",
